<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Le Locle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Le Locle is a town in the Jura mountains of the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, near the French border. Watchmaking took root there around 1700, traditionally through Daniel JeanRichard, and it became with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-chaux-de-fonds">La Chaux-de-Fonds</a> a world centre of the industry; the two towns are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Watch firms such as Tissot and Zenith are based there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-locle.jpg" alt="Le Locle" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Zumbühl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Locle_Kath_Kirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
